# Vendored SDK binaries must never be treated as text (autocrlf would corrupt
# them on checkout/clone, breaking ctypes loading at deploy time).
*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.lib binary

# The photosphere simulator's committed fixture cases are hashed bytes, not
# text: `manifest.json` carries a SHA-256 over every frame PNG and over
# `observations.jsonl`, and `scores.json` copies that digest into `input_hash`
# rather than recomputing it. This repository runs with `core.autocrlf=true`,
# so without this line a fresh Windows clone would check the JSON and JSONL
# out with CRLF, and every one of those hashes would name bytes that are no
# longer on disk. Nothing would go red: the replay parses the files either
# way. The integrity claim would simply become false, which is worse.
# `-text` rather than `binary` so the README and the JSON still diff.
tools/photosphere_sim/fixtures/** -text

# The server's recorded JSON fixtures are graded as bytes (#445): a test
# records a route's answer, the engine's state or a case table, and compares
# the file with what the code serves now. Under `core.autocrlf=true` a fresh
# Windows clone checks them out with CRLF, and such a grade goes red on a
# correct server at its first byte (`b'\r' != b'\n'`); two tests had to learn
# to normalise before this line existed (`test_flows_wizard_door_answers.py`,
# `test_s5_recorded_state.py::_on_disk`), and each new recorder would have had
# to remember. `eol=lf` rather than `-text`: it also normalises a fixture an
# editor saved with CRLF back to LF on commit, and it cannot change what a
# JSON file means, because JSON keeps no raw CR inside a string (it is
# escaped as `\r`), so only the whitespace between tokens is touched.
server/tests/fixtures/**/*.json eol=lf
